<b>Ballot proposal: Constitutional Convention</b>

<p/>
<b>What is this and why?&nbsp; <i>(Non-partisan summary)</i></b></br>
The Constitution of the state of Michigan requires that, 
every 16 years, the voters must be offered a choice to
completely rewrite the Constitution.&nbsp;
This choice is "State Proposal 1" on the November 2026 ballot.
<p/>
The Constitution can and has been frequently amended,
e.g. the successful anti-gerrymandering amendment of 2018,
and the reproductive freedom amendment of 2022.&nbsp;
However, a Yes vote on this ballot question would 
start a process to rewrite the <b>entire</b> constitution.

<p/>
<b>How would it work?</b><br/>
If the voters say "Yes", it starts a 3-stage process.
<ol>
<li><b>Elect delegates.</b>&nbsp; Voters would elect convention delegates, starting
with a primary in Feb 2027, and then a general election in May 2027.&nbsp;
Voters would select one delegate for their state house district,
<b>and</b> one for their state senate district.&nbsp;
(So everyone gets to vote for <b>two</b> delegates.)

<li><b>Write the new constitution.</b>&nbsp; The convention would start in October 2027.&nbsp;
  There is no time limit.&nbsp; The last convention (1963) took 10 months to complete.&nbsp;
  Under current law, delegates would be paid a maximum (total) of $7500, 
  although the Legislature could raise that number.&nbsp;
  The proposed new constitution must be passed by a majority vote of the delegates.

<li><b>Voters accept or reject.</b>&nbsp; The voters then get final say on approving the new constitution.&nbsp;
   A special election will be held, within 90 days of the completion of
   of the new constitution.&nbsp;
   Voters get a simple Yes / No choice whether to adopt the new constition.

</ol>

<p/>
<b>Pros</b><br/>
<ul>
<li>Some argue that the current constitution and amendments have grown large and unwieldy, 
    and have had unintended consequences.</li>
<li>Others believe that the existing amendment process requires either large amounts of money,
    or very large, highly organized volunteer groups -- which some call "less democratic."</li>
</ul>
<br/>


<b>Cons</b><br/>
<ul>
<li>The <b>entire</b> constitution would be up for grabs at a convention.&nbsp;
  Existing rights could simply disappear, such as the prohibition
  on the death penalty, the independent citizens redistricting commission, 
  abortion rights, the ability for citizens to <b>amend</b> the constitution,
  or even the "Headlee" caps on property taxes.
</li>

<li>The election of the delegates, and the final approval of a new constitution,
is arguably "less democratic."&nbsp; They require 3 special elections,
which get far <b>fewer</b> voters than the November general election.&nbsp;
(Normal constitutional amendments are voted on in the November general elections.)
</li>

<li>"Dark money", including out-of-state PACs, could have an out-sized effect on the special election for the delegates.
</li>
<li>Michigan is a very "purple" state -- so the majority of delegates could be either Democratic
   <b>or</b> Republican.
</li>
</ul>
<br/>

<b>Who's in favor?&nbsp; Who's opposed?</b><br/>
<ul>
<li><b>In favor:</b> Republican state representative &amp; Speaker Matt Hall, 
     and Republican state senator Aric Nesbitt.&nbsp;
     Matt Hall is known for supporting Trump's economic &amp; trade policies, tariffs, and the war with Iran.
<li><b>Opposed:</b> 
an unusual coalition of both right and left-leaning politicians and organizations:
  <ul>
  <li>Republicans John Engler (former Governor), Bill Schuette (former Attorney General),
      Right-to-Life Michigan, the Michigan Chamber of Commerce, and the Michigan Catholic Conference.
  <li>The Michigan Democratic Party, the Michigan AFL-CIO, the Michigan Education Association,
       The League of Women Voters of Michigan, 
       and the Michigan League of Conservation voters.
  </ul>
</ul>


<p>&nbsp;</p>
<b>Conclusion&nbsp; <i>(Partisan: Democratic party)</i></b><br>
The Constitution can and has been amended by the people of Michigan through overwhelmingly
popular initiatives, including the anti-gerrymandering amendment of 2018, 
and the reproductive freedom amendment of 2022. 

<p/>
A "<b>NO</b>" vote will prevent special interests – using dark money – from exploiting a confusing, 
chaotic process to rewrite the entire constitution, 
dealing a potentially catastrophic blow to our state’s democracy. 

